From 77815467ed3597973b3fc03716987c821a33725b Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Orange Octo Treasury Engineer <treasury-engineer@orange-octo.test>
Date: Thu, 21 May 2026 11:39:01 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(client): ExternalKeyProvider SPI for HSM-backed
 subscribers
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Adds a new SPI that lets callers install A005 / X002 / E002 key material
from outside the JVM heap — typically a SunPKCS11 PrivateKey rooted in an
HSM or smartcard — without the library generating fresh in-process key
pairs or persisting private keys to disk.

Surface:
- New org.kopi.ebics.client.ExternalKeyProvider interface with three
  methods (a005(), x002(), e002()) returning a small (PrivateKey,
  X509Certificate) record per role.
- New User constructor accepting the SPI. Skips createUserCertificates()
  entirely so no in-process KeyPairGenerator runs. CertificateManager
  stays null, and saveUserCertificates() becomes a no-op for the lifetime
  of this user — external keys are the caller's persistence responsibility.
- New EbicsClient.createUser(..., ExternalKeyProvider) overload that
  delegates to the new constructor. The legacy
  createUser(...PasswordCallback) overload is unchanged and continues to
  generate + PKCS#12-persist as before.

Provider routing:
- User.sign() / authenticate() previously hardcoded BouncyCastle, which
  caused InvalidKeyException for SunPKCS11-rooted private keys. Replaced
  with a small newSignature(algo, key) helper that resolves the JCE
  Provider via the key's class name (sun.security.pkcs11.* → SunPKCS11-*
  provider) and falls back to BouncyCastle for legacy software keys.
- User.decrypt() gets the same treatment for the E002 RSA cipher.
- Behavioural compatibility: any caller using the legacy in-process flow
  hits the BouncyCastle fallback unchanged.

Tests:
- ExternalKeyProviderTest exercises KeyMaterial guards, the new User
  constructor, and verifies the legacy BouncyCastle path still produces
  a valid 256-byte signature for software-backed external keys.
- HSM-specific routing (SunPKCS11 → on-token signing) is verified by
  downstream consumers against SoftHSMv2 — out of scope for unit tests.

Motivation: enables EBICS subscribers whose private keys never leave a
PKCS#11 device. Without this seam, the only way to drive INI/HIA with an
HSM-backed signer is to fork the library, since createUser() and User()
both go through CertificateManager.create() → KeyUtil.makeKeyPair() and
the in-process key is then signed against a BouncyCastle Signature
instance that does not accept opaque SunPKCS11 keys.
